{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310802,"nonce":"jbSlIQKZEaU5QjxgJW/rrnq95BQ=","title":"Mistral-7B peak memory levers, MEASURED not guessed: KV cache lazy-sizing is a DEAD END (~15 MB, calloc-lazy), macOS ...","message":"rayzor peak 4870 MB vs llama.cpp 4309 MB on a 4166 MB model = **704 MB of\nnon-model overhead against their 143 MB**. Region split during decode\n(`vmmap -summary`, dirty + swapped):\nMALLOC_LARGE 337 MB · MALLOC_SMALL 202 MB · **MALLOC_LARGE (empty) 139 MB**.\n\nThe cache is 305-336 MB, eagerly sized for full ctx at load (4 allocations per\nlayer x 32 layers: K data 4.25 MB, V data 4.25 MB, K scales 0.52, V scales 0.52\n— confirmed by resolving the allocation stacks). It LOOKS like a 230 MB lever.\nIt is not: `Bytes.alloc` -\u003e `alloc_zeroed` -\u003e calloc, so untouched pages never\nmaterialise. **MEASURED: ctx 4096 -\u003e 2048 -\u003e 1024 moves peak 710 -\u003e 700 -\u003e 695\nMB.** Pages arrive at ~84 KB/token as rows are written.\nSTILL WORTH KNOWING: `maxCtx = 0` means \"use the GGUF's own context_length\",\nand mistral-7b-instruct-v0.2 declares **32768** -\u003e ~2.6 GB of reservation for\nany caller that does not pass `--ctx`. The examples pass 4096.\nAnd never fall back to the F32 cache (`NUE_KV_Q8=0`): 1024 MB, eagerly memset.\n\n`Mem.releaseFreePages(goalKb)` shipped (adf5bc59 + 64cedb1b) and **reclaims\nnothing on macOS 26.6**. Tried NULL zone, the explicit default zone, and every\nzone from `malloc_get_all_zones`; against 250 MB of freed pooled 64 KB blocks\nand against a live 7B decode holding 153 MB in freed regions. Always 0 KB.\nWhy: libmalloc already munmaps LARGE blocks on free(), so what it retains is\nthe SMALL-OBJECT POOL, and it will not hand that back on request. **On macOS\nthe only way to shrink that pool is to stop filling it** — i.e. the per-token\nchurn (1.7M live small allocations), not the allocator.\nKept for glibc/NUC, which retains harder and does honour `malloc_trim`.\n\n`NUE_AMX_MIN_BATCH=999999` (AMX prefill off) moves peak overhead\n**679 -\u003e 529 MB**, at the cost of ttft 26.8 -\u003e 29.4s. A 112 MiB block\n(= 14336 x 4096 x 2, an f16 FFN weight) is visible in the heap census.\n**It is NOT `Q4Matmul._w16`.** PROVED by probe, not inferred: a\n`releasePrefillScratch()` hook on the decode path was reached 20,026 times in\nan 8-token run and `_w16`/`_x16` were null EVERY time, despite `amxPrefill()`\nand `haxeAmx()` both defaulting on. Hook reverted (2fa7ac6a) rather than ship\nsomething that never fires. **Find the real owner before freeing anything** —\nlikely the plugin/Rust side (`qw.matmulXTQThreaded`), whose own doc says it\n\"kept a permanent f16 copy per weight\".\n\n- Peak != steady state. Peak lands DURING prefill, so freeing after prefill\n  cannot lower it — only tiling the dequant into row blocks would.\n- `footprint -p PID` (phys_footprint) unprivileged; RSS understates.\n- Resolve big blocks by size class from `heap`, then find the matching subtree\n  in `malloc_history -callTree -invert` (depth-2 children).\n- ALWAYS probe with a print before believing a code path runs. Two separate\n  wrong attributions this session came from assuming a default-on gate meant\n  the path executed.\nSee [[perf_anonymous_heap_attributed]], [[perf_7b_busy_laptop_pressure_robustness]].\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): 704 MB overhead vs their 143. DEAD ENDS: KV lazy-sizing, macOS malloc trim. TRAP: `maxCtx=0` ⇒ ctx 32768 ⇒ 2.6 GB.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_peak_memory_levers_measured` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}